# Netlify redirects for trydibbs.com. Netlify reads this file BEFORE netlify.toml,
# top to bottom, first match wins. Design notes: docs/DEPLOY-WEB.md section 5.
#
# The site is a single-page app: one app shell (index.html), and the router in
# the browser draws each screen. The host must therefore hand the app shell to
# every real route, but it must NOT do that for URLs that are not routes, or
# every typo and every missing file (/manifest.json, /wp-login.php) reports
# "200 OK" and Google files it as a soft 404. So: list the real routes, and
# end with a 404.

# 1. One site, one address: https://trydibbs.com. Each old or alternate host
# goes to the bare domain in a single hop. These stay at the top. They only
# fire once those hostnames are attached to this Netlify site.
https://app.trydibbs.com/*  https://trydibbs.com/:splat  301!
https://www.trydibbs.com/*  https://trydibbs.com/:splat  301!
http://app.trydibbs.com/*   https://trydibbs.com/:splat  301!
http://www.trydibbs.com/*   https://trydibbs.com/:splat  301!

# 2. Real files are served by Netlify before any rule below (no "!" on the
# rewrites), so /_expo/*, /assets/*, /favicon.ico, /robots.txt and so on never
# reach this list. That includes dist/404.html (the shell with noindex).

# 3. Prerendered public pages (scripts/prerender-public.ts writes
# dist/<page>/index.html with its own title, description and text). The "!"
# serves the file at the clean URL with a 200 and stops Netlify from bouncing
# /about to /about/. The canonical tag in each file names the slash-less URL.
/about          /about/index.html          200!
/why-dibbs      /why-dibbs/index.html      200!
/faq            /faq/index.html            200!
/legal/terms    /legal/terms/index.html    200!
/legal/privacy  /legal/privacy/index.html  200!

# 4. Every other real route in app/ is the app shell with a 200. Keep this list
# in step with the app/ directory: when a screen is added, add its URL here
# (docs/SEO.md, "Adding a public page"). Dynamic routes use a splat.
/                          /index.html  200
/sell                      /index.html  200
/community                 /index.html  200
/profile                   /index.html  200
/welcome                   /index.html  200
/rules                     /index.html  200
/inbox                     /index.html  200
/saved                     /index.html  200
/settings                  /index.html  200
/moderation                /index.html  200
/create-profile            /index.html  200
/concepts/*                /index.html  200
/auth/*                    /index.html  200
/listing/*                 /index.html  200
/user/*                    /index.html  200
/conversation/*            /index.html  200
/reservation/*             /index.html  200
/payments/*                /index.html  200
/dibbs-offer               /index.html  200
/dibbs-offer/*             /index.html  200
/staff/*                   /index.html  200

# 5. Anything else is a real 404. Netlify serves /404.html with that status.
# 404.html is the same app shell plus <meta name="robots" content="noindex">,
# so a person who lands on a route missing from section 4 still gets a working
# app (the router shows its own not-found or the right screen); only the HTTP
# status code differs. A missed route therefore costs a status code, not a
# broken page.
/*  /404.html  404
